@1 = ~(The wyvern rests beside what remains of the dire wolf. Though still terribly weak, she no longer looks as though death might claim her at any moment. Her breathing is slow and steady, and she pays you no mind.)~
@2 = ~(The wyvern raises her head as you approach. She shows no hostility. She is far too weak for that.)~
@3 = ~(Feed the wyvern the dire wolf's carcass.)~
@4 = ~(End the wyvern's suffering.)~
@5 = ~(Leave the wyvern.)~
@6 = ~(As you enter the cave, you glimpse a wyvern. Your reflexes nearly drive you to attack, but the creature makes no threatening move. You cautiously draw nearer. She is lying in a pool of her own blood, with a clutch of eggs gathered close to her body. At least one egg appears damaged. Her exhausted eyes follow your movements, pleading rather than menacing. Something has gone terribly wrong.)~
@7 = ~(The wyvern cannot muster even a hiss. She watches you with dull, frightened eyes.)~
@8 = ~(Feed the wyvern the dire wolf's carcass.)~
@9 = ~(End the wyvern's suffering.)~
@10 = ~(Leave the wyvern.)~
@11 = ~Cernd, could we feed her something?~
@12 = ~(The wyvern lowers her head onto the stone. Her breaths come slowly.)~
@13 = ~(Feed the wyvern the dire wolf's carcass.)~
@14 = ~(End the wyvern's suffering.)~
@15 = ~(Leave the wyvern.)~
@16 = ~(You decide to end the poor creature's suffering. You lift a heavy stone from the cave floor. The wyvern gazes at you, unable to move or resist. You bring the stone down with one swift, decisive blow. Her passing is immediate; at last, her pain is over.)~
@17 = ~(You decide to leave the poor creature to her fate. The wyvern regards you with clouded eyes, though you cannot tell whether she welcomes your choice or despairs of it. She lowers her head and pays you no further attention.)~
@18 = ~(You place the dead dire wolf beside the wyvern, careful to avoid sudden movements. With immense effort, she crawls towards the carcass. She looks at you for a moment, then begins to feed. You slowly retreat to avoid startling her. As you watch the mother and her clutch, the tragedy becomes clear: one of the eggs contains a dead hatchling. Hunger, stress and the poor nesting conditions may have caused egg binding: she could not pass an egg normally, and the hatchling inside suffocated. The ordeal has left her close to death.)~
@19 = ~(The meal may give her enough strength to fall into a long, restorative sleep, easing some of her exhaustion. You have done this mother a kindness. Whether or not she can ever express it, she seems to know.)~
@20 = ~The poor creature has suffered severe malnourishment. She is utterly exhausted, hardly able to hiss at a threat. Approaching carelessly might still provoke her, but judging by the state of that egg, she has either lost a hatchling or seen it taken from her.~
@21 = ~Sunken eyes, weakness, tattered wing membranes... a dreadful case of malnourishment. Without food, this poor creature will surely die. Yet suffering and death have their place in nature. I fear we ought to let nature take its course.~
@22 = ~Severe hunger has left her barely able to digest food. A great meal might overwhelm her, though a smaller one could help. Still, her condition seems to have arisen through natural causes. I would hesitate to interfere with that balance. I urge us to leave her be.~
@23 = ~Ach! That overgrown dragon wannabe is kickin' the bucket! The bloody world's comin' to an end. Can we move along already?!~
